rn: with the presolve having applied pre_load (tip force -1e5), the DC solve's model still has pre_load... In the current test, the DC solve passes `slave_load` as the load case — the proportional term is slave_load only. But the model has both load cases added; solve uses the given load_case. The initial state has internal force from pre_load equilibrium: f_int(u0) = lambda_pre * F_pre. In the new solve, external = lambda * F_slave. The residual f_int - lambda*F_slave is NOT zero at the initial state — the initial state is not in equilibrium for the new load! \n\nHmm. With equilibrate_initial_state=False, the solve starts from a non-equilibrated state (imbalance = lambda_pre * F_pre). The DC Newton will absorb this: the first step's solve finds (q, lambda) such that control = first target AND reduced residual = 0. The imbalance from pre_load gets absorbed... into lambda? Only the F_slave direction can be absorbed. The pre_load force is at the tip shell node uz; F_slave is at the slave beam uz. These are different dofs — the imbalance cannot be fully absorbed by lambda alone; the structure must deform to equilibrate. That's fine — it's a genuine nonlinear solve; the first step from a non-equilibrium initial state. Newton should handle it if the imbalance isn't too large relative to the step... The imbalance is the full pre_load internal force (1e5 N at tip). The first step target moves only -4e-6. Newton must find q that both equilibrates the 1e5 N (i.e., stays near the presolved shape) and satisfies the control. Since the presolved state nearly satisfies it, Newton converges to a nearby state with the new control value. lambda ends up absorbing the component... Actually the equilibrium at solution: f_int(q) = lambda*F_slave. The presolved f_int = lambda_pre*F_pre. These are different load distributions! f_int(q_final) must be parallel to F_slave (single dof). The structure must deform from the presolved state to a state where internal force is a single point force 