{"id": "reasoning-3d276222-a09c-5765-a13b-4c2ea1f8fddc-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792347650972, "updatedAt": 1792347650972, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "support_reactions: dict name → 6-tuple (sum of reaction forces by component at all dofs of that BC). For the EP model, BC \"root\" covers the root edge nodes (all 6 dofs each). The reaction 6-vector = sum of forces (fx,fy,fz) and moments? No — components are dof components (ux..rz), so values are sums per component: [sum Fx, sum Fy, sum Fz, sum Mx, sum My, sum Mz] where the \"moments\" are the rotational dof reactions (generalized). That's a \"wrench\" only in the generalized sense (not moment about a point — the rz/ry/rx entries are the nodal rotational reactions, not moments of forces about a reference point).\n\nForce/wrench balance: external applied load = lambda * F_prop (tip nodal forces fz at NY+1 nodes). Force balance: sum(reaction[0:3]) + lambda*sum(F_prop[0:3]) ≈ 0. Moment balance about a point: need nodal reaction positions. But support_reactions gives only component sums, not per-node. To do a true wrench balance I'd need per-node reactions — not available in the summary. \n\nAlternative: the rotational reaction components ARE the nodal moment reactions (generalized moments at nodes). Moment balance about origin: sum over nodes (r_i × f_i + m_i) + applied (r×f) = 0. Without per-node data, I can't compute r×f for reactions... unless I recompute reactions myself: reaction = K... Actually I can compute the full residual imbalance myself: f_int(u) - lambda*F_prop at the final state gives support reactions at supported dofs (elsewhere ~0). Using _assemble_nonlinear_system (as in independent_residual) I get f_int; then reaction_full = f_int - lambda*F_prop; at free dofs this ≈ 0 (equilibrium), at support dofs = reactions. Then wrench balance: sum over ALL dofs of reaction_full should be ≈ 0 for force balance (since at free dofs residual≈0 and at supports the reaction balances...). Hmm wait: force balance means sum of all external forces = 0: applied tip loads + support reactions = 0. Support reactions = -(f_int - lambda F_prop) at support dofs... Let me define: equilibrium: f_int(u) = lambda*F_prop + R where R has entries only at supported dofs (the support forces). So R = f_int - lambda*F_prop (nonzero only at supports, ≈0 elsewhere). Force balance: sum(R[uz dofs]) + lambda*sum(F_prop[uz]) = 0 — trivially true by construction? No — R is defined as the imbalance, so at FREE dofs imbalance = 0 (equilibrium) and at SUPPORT dofs R = reaction. Total force balance: sum over all dofs of (R + lambda*F_prop) = 0? At free dofs R+lambda F_prop = f_int ≈ ... hmm, this is getting circular.\n\nTrue force balance check: the structure is in equilibrium under applied loads + support reactions. Sum of applied forces (lambda * tip loads) + sum of support reactions = 0. Support reactions at the root BC = -(imbalance at support dofs)... sign conventions. From _support_reaction_resultants: reaction_imbalance = reaction_internal - (reaction_constant + lam*reaction_proportional), i.e., f_int - f_ext at support dofs = R (the force the support exerts... or its negative). Then force balance: sum(R) + lambda*sum(F_prop) should = 0 if f_int sums to ~0 (self-equilibrated internal forces — true for a proper discretization: sum of internal force components over all dofs = 0 since element internal forces are self-equilibrated... for shells with rotational dofs, the translational sum is zero exactly by element equilibrium).\n\nSo: force balance: R_root[0:3] + lambda * F_tip[0:3] ≈ 0. That's checkable from support_reactions[\"root\"] and the applied load. Wrench (moment about origin): sum over nodes of (r_i × f_i) = 0 for internal forces (self-equilibrated) → moment balance: moment of reactions about a point + moment of applied loads = 0. But support_reactions sums rotational dof reactions (generalized moments m_i) and force components separately; moment of reaction FORCES about a point needs per-node positions... \n\nBUT: the rotational dof reactions at each node ARE the nodal moments; for the wrench balance about the origin: M_total = sum_i (r_i × R_i + m_i) + applied moments. Since I only have component sums, I can't form r_i × R_i.\n\nAlternative: compute the wrench balance myself from the full residual vector: wrench_balance = sum over nodes of (r_i × f_i) where f = imbalance + lambda*F_prop... hmm.\n\nActually — simpler and rigorous: compute the full imbalance vector g = f_int(u) - lambda*F_prop (via _assemble_nonlinear_system, deep-copied states). At free dofs |g| <= tolerance (that's the independent residual, already asserted). At support dofs g = reactions. Force balance: total sum: sum over translational dofs of g + lambda*F_prop... \n\nLet me think about what \"force/wrench balance\" the supervisor wants: \"Include explicit force/wrench balance and deep state immutability in final assertions, not just output dictionary fields.\" (from EP diagnostic review). I think the intent: verify that reactions balance the applied loads: net force = 0 and net moment about a point = 0. \n\nI can do this properly by computing per-node reactions myself:\n- g = f_int(u, states) - lambda * F_prop (full vector).\n- For each node: force f_i = g[translational dofs of node i], moment m_i = g[rotational dofs].\n- For free nodes, f_i ≈ 0, m_i ≈ 0 (equilibrium residual).\n- For support (root) nodes: f_i, m_i = reactions.\n- Force balance: sum_i f_i + lambda * sum_i F_prop_translational = 0? No wait — g already includes -lambda*F_prop. Total external force on structure = support reactions + applied = (-g at supports?) ... \n\nSigns: equilibrium: f_int = f_ext + f_support, so g = f_int - f_ext = f_support at support dofs (and 0 at free dofs). f_support is the force the support applies to the structure. Force balance of structure: f_support + f_ext = f_int... no. Newton on structure: f_ext + f_support - f_int = 0 → f_support = f_int - f_ext = g. OK so g IS the support force (nonzero only at supports). Force balance: sum(g_translational) + sum(f_ext_translational) = 0? sum over all dofs: sum(f_int) = 0 (self-equilibrated) → sum(g) = -sum(f_ext)... wait sum(g) = sum(f_int) - sum(f_ext) = 0 - lambda*sum(F_prop) = -lambda*sum(F_prop). And force balance says sum(f_support) + sum(f_applied) = 0 → sum(g) + lambda*sum(F_prop) = 0. ✓ consistent. So the force balance check: sum over translational components of g + lambda*sum(F_prop_translational) ≈ 0 — but this is only as good as sum(f_int)=0 exactly (discretization property, should hold to machine precision for translational components).\n\nMoment balance about origin: sum_i (r_i × g_i,trans + g_i,rot) + lambda * sum_i (r_i × F_prop,i,trans + F_prop,i,rot) ≈ 0. This uses per-node g — computable! And it's a genuine wrench balance: internal forces are self-equilibrated in wrench too (shell element internal force wrench sums to zero over the element... for a discretization, sum over all dofs of (r×f + m) of f_int = 0 — yes, internal virtual work against rigid modes = 0 for properly formulated elements including shell drilling/rotations... shells with 6 dofs: rigid rotation mode gives sum(r×f + m) = 0 exactly if the element is objective. This is exactly the kind of check the supervisor wants.)\n\nSo the wrench balance: W = sum_i (r_i × g_i + m_i) + lambda * sum_i (r_i × p_i + pm_i) where p = F_prop per node. Assert |W| small relative to lambda*|applied wrench|. Since g at free dofs is already <= residual tolerance (1e-7 relative), the balance is dominated by exactness of internal wrench = 0. Tolerance: relative to applied wrench magnitude, say 1e-6? Let me set: |W| <= 1e-6 * |applied wrench| — hmm, need to be careful not to over-assert. The independent residual gate is 1e-7 relative; the wrench balance should hold to similar level. I'll use 1e-6 relative to be safe, and calibrate if it fails (but avoid sweeps — one observation is fine).\n\nAlso simpler force balance from support_reactions dict: R = support_reactions[\"root\"] (6 sums). force balance: R[0:3] + lambda*sum(F_prop[0:3]) ≈ 0. This uses the output field — supervisor said \"not just output dictionary fields\" — meaning don't ONLY use output fields; include explicit balance. I'll do the wrench computation from the independently assembled residual (not output fields), which is stronger.\n\nDeep state immutability: after solves, deep-compare the caller's input initial_displacements/initial_element_states (deep copies made before the call) against post-call copies. The solver has _owned_initial_displacements (copies) — but the test should verify from outside.\n\nNow — the independent residual at actual lambda: from ep_edge_coupling.independent_residual — but note the supervisor's review: \"ep_edge_coupling.independent_residual fixes lam=1 ... DC must reconstruct at its achieved unknown load factor and normalize consistently\". The current independent_residual(model, load_case, u, states) computes ref_norm = |reduce_force(u, F_prop)| and resid = |reduce_force(u, 1.0*F_prop - F_int)| — wait, lam_force(1.0) = reduce_force(u, F_prop - F_int)?? That's wrong: the residual at achieved lambda should be F_int - lambda*F_prop. Hmm: `lam_force = lambda lam: reduce_force(u, lam*F_prop - F_int)` and they call lam_force(1.0) → reduce_force(u, F_prop - F_int) = -(reduced F_int - F_prop). Hmm — that's the residual assuming lambda=1. But DC's achieved lambda ≠ 1! The supervisor flagged exactly this: \"independent_residual fixes lam=1 and dc_summary calls it without res.load_factor. DC must reconstruct at its achieved unknown load factor\".\n\nSo in my test I must compute: resid = |reduce_force(u, F_int - lambda*F_prop)| / ref_norm where lambda = res.load_factor, ref_norm = |reduce_force(u, F_prop)| (or max(...,1)). Gate: <= 1e-7.\n\nWait, but for the RESUMED run: res.load_factor is the new solve's lambda (absorbing prior state). The final equilibrium: f_int(u_final) = lambda_res * F_prop (the new solve's external). Is that right? The resumed solve's external load = lambda*F_prop only (no constant). At its final state, equilibrium holds with ITS lambda. So yes, use res.load_factor of each run.\n\nHmm wait, actually — is that true? The resumed solve starts from the half state (equilibrium at lambda_half*F_prop) with lambda starting at 0. Its steps ramp control -0.10→-0.20; at each accepted step, equilibrium: f_int = lambda_new*F_prop. At the final state, f_int(u2_final) = lambda2*F_prop. And the uninterrupted: f_int(u3_final) = lambda3*F_prop. Same u → lambda2 == lambda3 (as I argued before). But the half's lambda_half ≠ lambda3/2 necessarily... whatever, not asserted.\n\nBut hold on — is the resumed run's initial state consistent? At the start of the resumed run, the state has f_int = lambda_half*F_prop but the solve's external at lambda=0 is zero. The first step solves for (q, lambda) with control at first target — Newton finds the equilibrium with the NEW lambda. Since the state is already near an equilibrium of the same load family (F_prop direction), the first step's Newton adjusts lambda from 0 to near... hmm, actually the first step target is -0.1167; the state must move from -0.10 (equilibrium at lambda_half) to -0.1167 (equilibrium at some lambda). Newton handles it. The supervisor said \"its augmented unknown absorbs the previous lambda without a production API change\" — confirming this works.\n\nNow — the load factor history comparison resumed vs uninterrupted: resumed steps' load_factor vs uninterrupted steps[6:] load_factor — should match closely (same control targets, same physics). Assert allclose rtol 1e-6? Calibrate.\n\nDisplacements comparison: max abs diff — calibrate. I'll assert <= 1e-6 initially.\n\nNow, one more thing to verify: does `solve_static_nonlinear` (public) accept initial_displacements etc.? The public wrapper at line ~8107. Let me check its signature quickly. Also check `emit_restart_checkpoint` default False — yes. And `record_increment_snapshots` default False — I'll pass True for the full runs to assert snapshot/step correspondence.\n\nAlso for the cancel test: progress_callback signature — emit_progress(progress_callback, \"nonlinear_static_step\", \"nonlinear_static.displacement\", completed=..., total=..., ...) — the callback receives an event object/dict? Let me check emit_progress and ProgressCallback.", "summary": []}
