{"id": "reasoning-4f815047-7502-530d-a543-8a27d38328d2-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792347678173, "updatedAt": 1792347678173, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The dc_full diagnostic failed at checkpoint emission (the known serialized Q4 checkpoint limitation) after ~18s — the solve itself ran. With emit_restart_checkpoint=False, that path is avoided.\n\nTiming estimate: the dc_full run took 18.2s including the solve and the failed checkpoint validation. So a 12-step DC solve ≈ 15s. My module with ~12 solves ≈ 150-200s. OK.\n\nNow, before writing the continuation module, let me fix the two fixtures in the existing module. Then run the existing module through the harness first (stable integrated check), then write the continuation module.\n\nActually — order per brief: \"First run focused source repair tests through harness and fix remaining fixture failures.\" So fix R1/R5 fixtures, run module, fix any failures. Then continuation module.\n\nLet me now write the R1 and R5 edits.\n\nR1 fix — replace the degenerate shell part:\n\nCurrent:\n```python\n    # Ordinary linear chain without any rotating descriptor still solves.\n    shell_only = FEModel(\"shell_only_chain\")\n    shell_only.add_material(\"steel\", 2.1e11, 0.3, density=7850.0)\n    shell_only.add_node(1, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)\n    shell_only.add_node(2, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0)\n    shell_only.add_node(3, 0.5, 0.1, 0.0)\n    shell_only.add_element(\n        1, create_shell_element(1, [1, 2, 3, 3], \"steel\", thickness=0.02)\n    )\n    sd = shell_only.mesh.dof_manager.get_node_dofs\n    shell_only.add_constraint_equation(\n        terms=((int(sd(3)[2]), 1.0), (int(sd(2)[2]), -1.0)),\n        rhs=0.0,\n        source_id=\"linear_chain\",\n        dependent_dof=int(sd(3)[2]),\n    )\n    shell_only.add_boundary_condition(\n        BoundaryCondition(\n            \"root\", [1, 2],\n            {name: 0.0 for name in (\"ux\", \"uy\", \"uz\", \"rx\", \"ry\", \"rz\")},\n        )\n    )\n    shell_only.apply_boundary_conditions()\n    load = LoadCase(\"tip\")\n    load.add_nodal_load(3, forces=(0.0, 0.0, -50.0))\n    shell_only.add_load_case(load)\n    _, _, T2, _, _, _ = _transformation(shell_only, load)\n    assert build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map(shell_only, T2) is None\n    result = solve_static_nonlinear(\n        shell_only, load, kinematics=\"corotational\", num_steps=1,\n        max_iterations=10,\n    )\n    assert result.status == \"completed\"\n```\n\nNew: valid 4-node rectangle [1,2,3,4] with nodes 1(0,0,0), 2(1,0,0), 3(1,0.2,0), 4(0,0.2,0); root on [1,4]; chain uz(3)=uz(2); load at node 3.\n\nWait — the constraint ties node 3 uz to node 2 uz, and load is at node 3. Node 3's uz is dependent; the load at a dependent dof projects through T onto node 2's uz. Fine.\n\nHmm, one thing: with root at [1,4] (x=0 edge fully fixed) and the tie uz3=uz2, the shell has one element; dofs: fine.\n\nR5 fix — rewrite test_r5_finite_state_admits_nonlinear_only_control_and_load:\n\n```python\ndef test_r5_finite_state_admits_nonlinear_only_control_and_load() -> None:\n    model, meta, pre_load, slave_load = _r5_model()\n    dofs = model.mesh.dof_manager.get_node_dofs\n\n    # Reach a genuine finite-rotation equilibrium with a dead shell load.\n    pre = solve_static_nonlinear(\n        model, pre_load, num_steps=6, max_iterations=25, tolerance=1.0e-9,\n        kinematics=\"corotational\", corotational_tangent=\"auto\",\n    )\n    assert pre.converged\n    pre_u = np.asarray(pre.displacements, dtype=float)\n    tip_master_ry = float(pre_u[dofs(meta[\"grid\"][(meta[\"nx\"], 0)])[4]])\n    assert tip_master_ry != 0.0\n\n    # The control coordinate is nonlinear-only: the linear row is exactly\n    # zero (tip master uz is fixed and the lever arm has no z component),\n    # while the finite-state kinematic Jacobian row and the proportional\n    # load projection are both nonzero, so admission succeeds at this state\n    # and fails at the zero-rotation origin.\n    K0, _ = assemble_stiffness_matrix(model)\n    F_prop, _ = assemble_load_vector(model, slave_load)\n    _, _, T, u0, _, _ = build_constraint_transformation(K0, F_prop, model)\n    mpc_map = build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map(model, T)\n    assert mpc_map is not None and mpc_map.active\n    control = DisplacementControl(\n        node_id=meta[\"beam_nodes\"][-1], dof=\"uz\", target_displacement=-0.01\n    )\n    row_full = control.full_row(model)\n    row_linear = np.asarray(row_full @ T, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n    assert float(np.linalg.norm(row_linear)) == 0.0\n    row_finite = np.asarray(\n        row_full @ mpc_map.jacobian(pre_u), dtype=float\n    ).reshape(-1)\n    assert float(np.linalg.norm(row_finite)) > 0.0\n    zero_u = np.zeros_like(pre_u)\n    proj_origin = mpc_map.reduce_force(zero_u, F_prop)\n    proj_finite = mpc_map.reduce_force(pre_u, F_prop)\n    assert float(np.linalg.norm(proj_origin)) == 0.0\n    assert float(np.linalg.norm(proj_finite)) > 0.0\n```\n\nWait — careful: `mpc_map.jacobian(pre_u)` — jacobian expects u_current = the mapped full state? In admission: initial_u = full_u(q) = apply_kinematics(T@q+u0) — the mapped state. pre.displacements is the mapped state from the solve (result displacements are physical/mapped). ✓. But subtlety: jacobian(u) computes theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u) — from the mapped state. ✓.\n\nHmm — wait, one more check on `reduce_force(zero_u, F_prop)`: at zero state, f_mod unchanged (delta_A=0), then T.T @ f. f has -5e3 at slave uz dof. T row slave_uz: slave_uz = 1.0*master_uz + (theta×r0)_z. In build_constraint_transformation's T, the master_uz is fixed → the free-column coefficient is 0, and the fixed contribution is in u0. So T[slave_uz, :] = 0 for all free columns → projection exactly 0. ✓.\n\nBut wait — actually is the slave uz row in T exactly zero? The lever arm term (theta_bar × r0)_z: theta_bar =