{"id": "reasoning-839bc61f-7e7a-5f7b-8421-eb8b197cb52d-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792347797711, "updatedAt": 1792347797711, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Interesting — the solve completed (all prior assertions passed: control error <= 1e-8, load_factor nonzero, inputs preserved), but the tip master ry changed only by 7e-6, not ~0.015 as I predicted. Why?\n\nThe control target moved slave uz by -8e-6. I assumed slave uz changes only via master rotation (master uz fixed). But wait — the slave uz = weighted master uz + (R-I)r|_z. Master tip uz is fixed... but which master? The tip beam node (slave) is at ix=nx → masters = tip master node. Its uz is fixed. So slave uz change must come from (cos ry - 1)e... but ry changed only 7e-6?? Then slave uz change from rotation = -e sin(ry)*Δry = -0.05*0.00314*7e-6 ≈ -1.1e-9. But the control moved -8e-6. Contradiction!\n\nUnless... the control value isn't slave uz at the tip. Wait — control node = meta[\"beam_nodes\"][-1] = the LAST beam node = tip beam slave (b_id 100+nx). Hmm. But wait — the initial_control = row_full @ pre_u = slave tip uz ≈ -2.47e-7. Target = -8.247e-6. The solve completed with |uz - target| <= 1e-8. So slave uz DID move -8e-6. But master ry barely moved. How?\n\nOh! I see — the eccentricity r_vec = (0,0,0.05) and the coupling: u_slave = u_master + (R-I)r. The z-component: e(cos ry - 1) requires ry change of ~0.018 to give -8e-6. But ry changed 7e-6. So the slave uz change came from somewhere else... \n\nWait, maybe the master uz is NOT actually fixed? The boundary condition \"fix_master_uz\" fixes tip_master = grid[(nx,0)] uz. The slave at ix=nx has master grid[(nx,0)]. Hmm.\n\nOR: the rotation that matters is theta_bar = weighted master rotation — the tip master's ry. It changed by 7e-6. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe I misread: maybe the control moved mostly by changing... the slave uz = master uz + (R-I)r_z. If master uz is fixed at 0... Let me reconsider: is the uz of the tip master actually fixed? In _r5_model: `model.add_boundary_condition(BoundaryCondition(\"fix_master_uz\", [tip_master], {\"uz\": 0.0}))` then `model.apply_boundary_conditions()`. But WAIT — _build_coupled_cantilever already called apply_boundary_conditions() at the end (with root_supports=True). Then _r5_model adds another BC and calls apply_boundary_conditions() again. Does the second call re-apply correctly (accumulate)? Presumably yes.\n\nHmm, so how did slave uz move -8e-6 with ry change 7e-6? Let me compute what slave uz change is consistent: Δslave_uz = Δmaster_uz + Δ[(R-I)r]_z. If Δmaster_uz = 0 (fixed): Δ[(R-I)r]_z = e(cos(ry_f) - cos(ry_i)) ≈ -e sin(ry)Δry = -0.05*0.00314*7e-6 ≈ -1.1e-9. Total ≈ -1.1e-9 ≠ -8e-6.\n\nUnless the master uz is NOT fixed in the reduced system... OR the control row picks a different dof. Hmm wait — actually let me reconsider: maybe apply_boundary_conditions() the second time REPLACES the fixed-dof set? If the second call re-applies all BCs (root + fix_master_uz), fine.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the \"beam_nodes[-1]\" slave... in the eccentric route, beam nodes are 100+ix for ix in 0..nx (nx+1 nodes). beam_nodes[-1] = 100+nx = tip slave. Its master = grid[(nx,0)] = tip master. OK.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe the answer: the DC control value `current` is computed as row_full @ full_u(q) — the mapped slave uz. The solve reported completed with |uz - target| <= 1e-8 where uz = result.displacements[slave uz dof]. So the final slave uz = -8.247e-6. And ry_final - ry_i = 7e-6.\n\nLet me recompute (R-I)r z-component more carefully. r = (0,0,0.05). R = rotation about y by ry (plus rx, rz components?). theta_bar = master rotation vector = (rx, ry, rz). The map uses rotation_matrix_from_vector(theta_bar) — full 3D. The z-component of (R-I)r for general theta: for small theta, (R-I)r ≈ theta × r = (theta_y*0.05, -theta_x*0.05, 0). So to first order the z-component is ZERO — it's second order: (cos ry - 1)*e ≈ -ry²e/2. At ry=0.00314: -2.5e-7. ✓ matches initial_control ≈ -2.47e-7.\n\nFor the z-component to reach -8.25e-6: ry² e/2 = 8.25e-6 → ry = sqrt(2*8.25e-6/0.05) = sqrt(3.3e-4) = 0.01817. But measured ry change is 7e-6! So the slave uz did NOT move via ry. Something else moved it.\n\nOH WAIT. I bet the master uz is NOT fixed — because... hmm. Or maybe the slave uz moved via rx? (R-I)r for rotation about x: R_x(rx) @ (0,0,e) = (0, -e sin rx, e cos rx). z-comp: e(cos rx - 1) — also second order. y-comp: -e sin rx.\n\nHmm, so how?? Unless the boundary condition on the master uz didn't take effect and master uz moved. Let me check: if master uz moved by -8e-6 (with tiny rotation change), that's consistent: slave uz ≈ master uz + second-order. So the \"fix_master_uz\" BC apparently did NOT fix the master uz in this second application... OR it did and I'm misreading which node.\n\nWait, actually — let me recheck _r5_model: tip_master = meta[\"grid\"][(meta[\"nx\"], 0)]. meta[\"nx\"] = 2. grid[(2,0)] = tip node at y=-0.1. The load pre_load is at grid[(nx,ny)] = grid[(2,1)] (y=+0.1). The control slave = beam_nodes[-1] = node 102, master = grid[(2,0)]. So control slave uz with master uz fixed...\n\nHmm, but the zero-rotation refusal test asserts \"zero control Jacobian\" — which requires master uz fixed (else linear row nonzero). That test passes. So master uz IS fixed.\n\nThen how did the control reach the target with ry change of only 7e-6?? Let me recompute the numbers: initial_control = row_full @ pre_u. What is pre_u[slave uz]? slave uz = master uz (0, fixed) + (R-I)r_z = e(cos ry - 1) ≈ 0.05*(1 - 0.99999507)/... ry=0.003144: ry²/2 = 4.94e-6; cos-1 = -4.94e-6; ×0.05 = -2.47e-7. So initial_control ≈ -2.47e-7, target ≈ -8.247e-6.\n\nFinal: uz = -8.247e-6 (assert passed within 1e-8). ry_final = 0.0031512. e(cos ry_f - 1) = -0.05*(0.0031512²/2) = -0.05*4.965e-6 = -2.48e-7. So slave uz from rotation ≈ -2.48e-7 — but actual is -8.25e-6! Difference: -8e-6 must come from master uz. So master uz moved by -8e-6. But it's \"fixed\"...\n\nUNLESS the DC control dof is treated as an unknown replacing... In DC formulation, the control NODE dof becomes free (the control equation replaces the... no). Hmm, wait — actually in this DC implementation, how is the control dof handled? The control row is added as an equation and lambda becomes unknown. The control dof (slave uz) is a dependent MPC dof — eliminated from q anyway. The control equation: row_full @ full_u(q) = target. The reduced system: K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = -residual; control row: J-row dq = target - current.\n\nSo the solve adjusts q (free dofs: master shell dofs except fixed ones) to move slave uz. With master uz fixed, only rotation moves slave uz (second order). To achieve -8e-6 via rotation alone needs ry ≈ 0.018. Measured ry change 7e-6 → slave uz change from rotation ≈ e·sin(ry)·Δry... = 0.05·0.00314·7e-6 = 1.1e-9. So the -8e-6 came from elsewhere: master uz must not be fixed.\n\nWait — maybe apply_boundary_conditions() called a second time doesn't add the new BC? Let me check FEModel.apply_boundary_conditions semantics. If the second call ignores new BCs because already applied... then master uz is free, and the linear row would be nonzero → the zero-rotation refusal test would fail. But it passes! Hmm — unless the refusal comes from the load projection instead... no, the match is \"zero control Jacobian\".\n\nHold on, actually let me reconsider: maybe the refusal test passes because... the J-row at zero: row_full @ J(0) = row_full @ T. If master uz were free, T[slave_uz, master_uz_col] = 1 → row nonzero → no refusal. Test passes → master uz IS fixed. \n\nSo in the finite-state test, master uz is fixed, and yet slave uz moved -8e-6 with negligible rotation change. Contradiction... unless the mapped slave uz isn't what I think. Let me recheck the assertion that passed: `uz = result.displacements[dofs(meta[\"beam_nodes\"][-1])[2]]` — beam_nodes[-1] = 102. `abs(uz - target) <= 1e-8` passed. So u[102_uz] = -8.247e-6.\n\nAnd `ry_final` measured at grid[(nx,0)] = master. Changed by 7e-6.\n\nHmm wait — maybe the presolve pre_load (constant) isn't in equilibrium and the first step redistributes: the constant pre_load is applied IN FULL from the first increment. The presolve ended at lambda=0.9999999999999999 ≈ 1. So initial state ≈ equilibrium. Fine.\n\nSo how can slave uz = -8.25e-6 with master uz = 0 (fixed) and ry = 0.00315? (R-I)r z-comp = -2.48e-7. Unless... rx or rz rotations contribute: (R-I)r for theta=(rx,ry,rz): z-comp = e(cos... for combined small rotations, z-comp ≈ -e(rx²+ry²)/2 + ... second order. rx could have changed! I only measured ry. If rx changed to... -e·rx²/2 = -8e-6 → rx² = 3.2e-4 → rx = 0.018. Possible! The presolve load at the +y edge tip node with -z force induces torsion (rx) on the strip! The control slave uz can move via rx² second-order term too. And the J-row includes d/drx = -e·sin(rx)·... at rx=0.003-ish similar magnitude.\n\nSo the control motion may have been carried by rx (torsion) instead of ry. My assertion `abs(ry_final - tip_master_ry) > 1e-3` was wrong-headed — the control moves through whatever rotational combination the solver picks; the regression point is admission + control achievement, not which rotation carries it.\n\nFix: assert the rotation VECTOR norm changed: |theta_final - theta_initial| > 1e-3? If it went via rx: rx change ~0.018 → norm change 0.018 > 1e-3 ✓. But is that guaranteed? The control moved -8e-6 via second-order rotation terms: e·Δ(theta²)/2 ≈ 8e-6 → |Δtheta| ≈ sqrt(2*8e-6/0.05) ≈ 0.018 (if from ~0). So the rotation norm change is ~0.018 regardless of which axis. Assert > 1e-3 is safe. Actually more robust: assert the full rotation vector (rx,ry,rz) change norm > 1e-3.\n\nAlternatively drop that assertion entirely — it's a sanity extra. But it does verify the control motion is physically carried by the nonlinear map (not by master translation, which is fixed). Keep it with the rotation vector norm.\n\nAlso — the master uz is fixed, so let me also assert master uz stayed 0 (guards that the control was carried by the map, not by violating the support): assert abs(u_final[master_uz]) == 0.0 (exactly, since fixed dofs are set exactly). Good discriminating assertion.\n\nLet me update:", "summary": []}
