 - 8e-6 where initial_control = row_full @ pre_u. What is row_full @ pre_u actually? If pre_u[slave_uz] ≈ -2.47e-7... but wait — is pre_u the MAPPED state? pre.displacements from the presolve — the solve returns the mapped physical state. Hmm.\n\nActually — wait. Maybe I should reconsider: perhaps the presolve pre_load state has significant slave uz! The presolve bends the strip: the tip master uz is FIXED at 0, but the presolve load is at the OTHER tip node (2,1). The slave beam runs along y=-0.1 (the masters are grid[(ix,0)] — the y=-0.1 edge). The tip master (2,0) uz fixed. The slave uz = master uz + (R-I)r_z = 0 + e(cos theta -1).\n\nHmm, but what is theta_bar at the tip master after presolve? ry = 0.003144 (measured). rx? torsion from the offset load — could be larger! If rx at tip master is, say, 0.05: (R-I)r|_z includes cross-terms... for theta with both rx and ry, (R-I)r z-comp ≈ e(cos φ - 1) where φ = |theta|... plus first-order terms? No — (R-I)r = R r - r; first-order term is theta × r = (theta_y e, -theta_x e, 0) — z-comp zero at first order for ANY theta. So z-comp is second order in |theta|: ≈ -e|theta|²/2.\n\nSo slave uz ≈ -e|theta|²/2. For the control to move -8e-6: Δ(|theta|²) = 2*8e-6/e = 3.2e-4. Measured Δtheta = 5.19e-4 with theta_i = |(rx,0.003144,rz)|. If theta_i is dominated by rx (say rx_i = 0.05): theta_i² = 2.5e-3; theta_f² = 2.5e-3+3.2e-4 → theta_f = sqrt(2.82e-3) = 0.0531 → Δtheta = 3.1e-3. But measured Δtheta = 5.19e-4?? Inconsistent again.\n\nUnless... the slave uz in the RESULT is not the mapped value? Hmm, wait — actually, maybe I've got it backwards: maybe the returned result.displacements is the LINEAR state (T@q+u0), not the mapped one? No — the R3 test asserts the returned state carries the mapped values (expected_u_b = u_bar + (rot-I)@r) and passes.\n\nLet me stop guessing and just print the actual numbers. Quick diagnostic: modify nothing; write a tiny scratch script that replicates the R5 fixture, runs the presolve + DC solve, and prints: pre_u at master/slave dofs (all 6 each), initial_control, target, final u at master/slave, theta vectors. Run through harness (all solves must go through harness). It's a small solve (~10s).\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider something. `initial_control = float(row_full @ pre_u)`. But the DC admission computes initial_control = row_full @ full_u(q) where q = reduced coordinates RECOVERED from initial_displacements. The recovery: initial_displacements → q via... the mapped state pre_u must be inverted (to_linear_displacement) then reduced. If pre_u is the mapped state, to_linear gives the linear state, then q = pinv stuff. full_u(q) should reproduce pre_u. OK.\n\nBut my `initial_control = row_full @ pre_u` — same thing if pre_u is mapped. ✓.\n\nSo target = pre_u[slave_uz] - 8e-6. Final slave_uz = target (within 1e-8). So slave_uz moved exactly -8e-6. And master uz = 0, theta change 5.19e-4.\n\nFor the mapped slave uz = master_uz + (R(theta)-I)r|_z with master_uz=0: the only way to move -8e-6 with |Δtheta|=5.19e-4 is if theta_i is large enough: Δ(theta²) = 2θΔθ = 3.2e-4 → θ_i = 3.2e-4/(2*5.19e-4) = 0.308! So the tip master rotation norm after presolve would be ~0.3 rad?? But ry = 0.00314. So rx ≈ 0.3?? Torsion of 0.3 rad from the presolve? The load -1e5 N at the +y tip node with the -y tip node uz fixed... that's a big torsional moment: 1e5 N × 0.1 m arm = 1e4 N·m on a strip with J_torsion... shell torsional stiffness. Plausible actually! The strip: W=0.2, t=0.02. Torsion constant J ≈ W t³/3 = 0.2*8e-6/3 = 5.3e-7. G = E/2.6 ≈ 8.1e10. GJ = 4.3e4. θ = ML/GJ... M varies... tip twist could be ~0.1-0.3 rad. And rx(torsion) at the tip master could be ~0.3!\n\nWait, but then the second-order term: e(cos... with theta ≈ 0.3: -e θ²/2 = -0.05*0.09/2 = -2.25e-3. Hmm, that would make initial slave uz ≈ -2.25e-3, not -2.47e-7.\n\nI'm clearly flailing. STOP. Print the actual numbers. Let me write a scratch diagnostic in the scratchpad... no wait — solves must go