the LINEAR state (T@q+u0) and the R3 test's assertion... no, R3 asserts mapped values in result.displacements and passes.\n\nHmm, one more possibility: the DC solve's returned displacements at the SLAVE dofs — the slave dofs are dependent; the returned vector includes slave values from the map. uz asserted = -8.247e-6 = target. ✓ by construction of the solve.\n\nAnd theta_change measured at the MASTER (grid[(2,0)]) rotation dofs: 5.19e-4. So slave_uz moved -8e-6 while master rotation moved 5.19e-4 and master uz = 0.\n\nMapped slave uz = master uz + [(R(theta)-I)r]_z. If master uz = 0 and theta change gives only ~1e-7... then mapped slave uz should have moved ~1e-7, but it moved 8e-6. CONTRADICTION with the map formula — unless theta_i is large (0.3) as computed. Let me test that hypothesis: theta_i ≈ 0.308, theta_f = theta_i + 5.19e-4 (norm). Δ(theta²)/2·e = e·theta_i·Δtheta = 0.05·0.308·5.19e-4 = 8.0e-6. ✓✓ EXACTLY -8e-6!\n\nSo the tip master rotation norm after presolve IS ~0.308 rad — dominated by rx (torsion) ≈ 0.308, with ry = 0.00314. The presolve twists the strip by ~0.3 rad! That's consistent: the load at the +y edge tip with the -y edge tip uz fixed creates a torsional couple; the strip is soft in torsion (open section), so 0.3 rad twist is plausible.\n\nSo everything is consistent — the state IS well-conditioned (theta ~0.3, J-row ~ e·sin(0.3) ≈ 0.015 per rad — actually much better conditioned than I estimated!). The control motion -8e-6 with sensitivity e·theta ≈ 0.05*0.3 = 0.015 → Δtheta = 8e-6/0.015 = 5.3e-4. ✓ matches measured 5.19e-4!\n\nSo the fixture is actually well-conditioned, the solve is clean, and my theta_change > 1e-3 assertion was just wrong by 2x. The right sanity assertion: theta_change > 0 (it moved through the rotation), or > 1e-4? Measured 5.19e-4. Assert > 1e-4 gives 5x margin. Or simply > 0.0. Given the control error is already asserted <= 1e-8 and master uz == 0 exactly, the physics is verified. I'll assert theta_change > 1e-4 (it must be ~Δtheta = increment/(e·theta_i); robust for this fixture).\n\nActually, to make it fully self-consistent without magic numbers: assert theta_change > 0.0 and additionally that the predicted lever-arm motion matches: |e·(theta_f² - theta_i²)/2 - (uz_f - uz_i)| small? That's re-deriving the map — overkill. Keep it simple: theta_change > 1e-4.\n\nAlso — now I understand the fixture better: the presolve state has ~0.3 rad torsion. That's a genuinely finite state. \n\nLet me update the assertion and rerun. Also fix the comment.", "summary": []}
