{"id": "reasoning-fe0afbef-f7c5-58db-a545-b10ec993b0fc-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792347913873, "updatedAt": 1792347913873, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "All 16 tests pass now (EXIT, wall 40.54s). Used 823.8s, remaining 2776.2s (reserve 900 → ~1876s non-reserve left).\n\nUpdate todos and move to the continuation test module. Let me now write tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation.py.\n\nDesign (from my earlier analysis):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Slice 3 continuation: EP edge-coupling product regressions (restart route).\"\"\"\n\nImports: copy, json?, numpy, pytest, anysolver API, anymaterial curve (via anysolver.material_curves like ep_isolation).\n\nModule constants: L=1.0, W=0.24, t=0.012, NX=2, NY=1, R=(0,0,0.04), CS dict.\nDC_PARAMS = num_steps=12, max_iterations=40, tolerance=1e-8.\n\ndef _material(model, scale): S355 curve like ep_isolation.\n\ndef _build_ep_model(coupling): returns model, load, probe, grid (direct node IDs, no coordinate search).\n\ndef _add_tip_load(m, lc, grid): -2000 N/node at ix=NX nodes.\n\ndef _dc_solve(coupling, target, num_steps, **extra): builds fresh model, adds tip load, DisplacementControl(probe, uz, target), solve with kinematics corotational, DC params, record_increment_snapshots=True, emit_restart_checkpoint=False.\n\nCache: _CACHE = {}\ndef _cached(key, factory): if key not in _CACHE: _CACHE[key] = factory(); return _CACHE[key]\n\nExpensive cached solves:\n- (\"full\", coupling): _dc_solve(coupling, -0.20, 12)\n- (\"half\", coupling): _dc_solve(coupling, -0.10, 6)\n- (\"resume\", coupling): fresh model, solve with initial_displacements=half.displacements (copy), initial_element_states=deepcopy(half.element_states), target -0.20, 6 steps, equilibrate_initial_state=False.\n- (\"reuse\", \"direct\"): same inputs again fresh → for cancel test reuse; compare to (\"resume\",\"direct\").\n\nTests:\n\n1. test_preyield_controls_direct/interp (or one test both): 4 solves (plastic/elastic × direct/interp) — cached as (\"preyield\",). Assert status completed, load_factor 1.0 (abs diff <= 1e-12), alpha_max == 0.0, uz_plastic == uz_elastic exactly.\n\n2. test_dc_full_direct / test_dc_full_interp: \n   - status completed, converged\n   - |uz + 0.20| <= 1e-8\n   - independent residual at actual lambda <= 1e-7 (relative)\n   - alpha_max > 0 and steps[-1].max_equivalent_plastic_strain > 0\n   - constraint postcheck: max_relative <= 1e-8? (check field names; use .get(\"max_relative\", 0.0) <= 1e-8 — need to verify actual value; maybe assert max_absolute <= 1e-9? I'll print in a probe... hmm. Actually constraint_residual_summary returns dict with max_absolute, max_relative, worst_origin, residual_l2 maybe. The EP dc_summary printed it. I don't have a sample value since dc_full failed before printing. Risk: unknown scale. I'll assert postcheck[\"max_relative\"] <= 1e-6 — hmm, \"existing constraint limits\" — the brief says \"existing constraint limits\". Maybe better to assert what the summary reports is small: max_absolute <= 1e-9 and max_relative <= 1e-9? Unknown. Safer: assert max_relative <= 1e-8. If it fails I'll see the actual.\n   - force/wrench balance from independently assembled residual: compute g = f_int(u, states) - lambda*F_prop; per-node wrench balance: sum_i (r_i × g_i,trans + g_i,rot) + lambda * applied wrench ≈ 0. Assert relative to applied wrench norm <= 1e-6.\n   - snapshots: len(snapshots) == len(steps), control values match steps.\n\n3. test_dc_split_direct / interp:\n   - half: completed, |uz+0.10|<=1e-8\n   - resume: completed, |uz+0.20|<=1e-8, independent residual at its lambda <= 1e-7\n   - resumed vs uninterrupted: max abs displacement diff <= 1e-6 (calibrate), final lambda relative diff, plastic strain history tail match.\n\n4. test_restart_input_immutability_and_reuse_direct:\n   - half result; deep copies of u/states; resume with cancellation after 2 accepted progress events → SolveCancelled; inputs unchanged (deep equal).\n   - reuse same inputs fresh → completed to -0.20; inputs unchanged again; result matches the cached (\"resume\",\"direct\") result (allclose tight, e.g., exact equality? deterministic same process — but the cached resume ran in the same process earlier... same inputs → identical result likely bit-exact. assert allclose atol 1e-12).\n   \n   Wait — but the cancelled run: does cancellation happen cleanly? progress_callback called after each accepted increment; cancel after 2 → token.cancel() → next safe point raises SolveCancelled. The solve raises SolveCancelled (RuntimeError). ✓ per ep diagnostic stage_dc_cancel design (which used status_callback — but supervisor said DC does not emit status_callback; use progress_callback).\n\n5. test_rejected_resume_refused_and_inputs_preserved:\n   - half result; corrupt initial_displacements to violate constraints (e.g., add 1e-3 to a constrained/master dof... which dof violates? The slave beam uz is dependent: u_slave = master + lever. Set u[slave_uz] += 1e-3 → violates MPC → ValueError \"incompatible with the active supports/MPC constraints\"). Deep copies before; assert ValueError raised; inputs unchanged after.\n   \n   Wait — but the input array I corrupt is MY array; the solver copies it. The immutability check: the array I passed is unchanged (the solver doesn't modify it in place). ✓.\n\n6. test_rejected_step_snapshots_exclude_rejected_history:\n   - a solve that stops without completing: e.g., resume from half with num_steps=1, max_iterations=1, tolerance=1e-8 → likely stopped. Assert status != completed; snapshots control values == steps control values (only accepted increments recorded); result.displacements corresponds to last accepted step (control value == steps[-1].control_value within 1e-8).\n   \n   Hmm — will max_iterations=1 actually fail? First iteration from a good state might converge in 1 iteration? At tolerance 1e-8, unlikely to converge in 1 iteration for a plastic step. But cutbacks: min_step_fraction 1/1024 → up to ~10 cutbacks per step, 1 iteration each = ~11 iterations, cheap. Status: \"stopped_at_limit\"? or \"failed\"? Assert status != \"completed\" and snapshots == accepted steps.\n\n   Risk: it might actually complete (if the first increment converges in 1 iteration — no). OK.\n\nAlso \"Nonlinear full route must be exercised\" — the DC solves are corotational + plasticity. ✓\n\nNow — the independent residual helper for the test:\n\n```python\ndef _independent_relative_residual(model, load, u, states, lam):\n    K0, _ = assemble_stiffness_matrix(model)\n    F_prop, _ = assemble_load_vector(model, load)\n    _, _, T, u0, _, _ = build_constraint_transformation(K0, F_prop, model)\n    mpc_map = build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map(model, T)\n    states_copy = copy.deepcopy(states)\n    f_int, _, _ = _assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u, states_copy, 5, tangent=False, kinematics=\"corotational\", corotational_tangent=\"work_conjugate\")\n    resid = mpc_map.reduce_force(u, f_int - lam*F_prop)\n    ref = mpc_map.reduce_force(u, F_prop)\n    ref_norm = max(norm(ref), 1.0)\n    return norm(resid)/ref_norm\n```\n\nWait — the supervisor's adopted formula: \"Independent residual at actual lambda<=1e-7\". resid = |J^T (f_int - lam F_prop)| / |J^T F_prop|. ✓.\n\nHmm — but careful: for the RESUMED run, is the equilibrium f_int = lam_new * F_prop? The resumed solve's external = lam*F_prop (no constant). ✓ use res.load_factor.\n\nBut WAIT — the model used for assembly in the resumed run: the model must be the one the resumed solve used (fresh model built the same way — identical). I'll keep the model object from the solve factory (return it in the cache).\n\nWrench balance:\n\n```python\ndef _wrench_balance(model, u, states, lam, load):\n    # recompute f_int (reuse from residual helper? separate call ok — assembly is cheap-ish; but _assemble_nonlinear_system with plasticity states — deepcopy states)\n    g = f_int - lam*F_prop  # support forces at supports, ~0 elsewhere\n    nodes: for each node id: r = (x,y,z); f = g[trans dofs]; m = g[rot dofs]\n    W = sum(r × f + m) + lam * sum over loaded nodes (r × p + pm)\n    applied wrench from load case: F_prop per node — but F_prop is a vector; per-node extraction same way.\n    assert norm(W_total) <= 1e-6 * norm(applied wrench)\n```\n\nHmm wait — sign: total external wrench (supports + applied) must be ~0: W = W_support + W_applied where W_support = sum(r×g + m) over ALL nodes (g≈0 at free nodes) and W_applied = lam * sum(r×p). Assert |W| <= 1e-6 * |W_applied|.\n\nBut is sum of internal force wrench exactly zero? For shell elements with rotational dofs — the internal virtual work against rigid-body modes is zero to machine precision IF the element formulation is objective/invariant. Corotational shell — should hold. The 1e-6 relative gate is safe.\n\nActually — also the FORCE balance: sum(g_trans) + lam*sum(p_trans) ≈ 0. Include both force and wrench (moment) balance. The wrench vector: I'll compute 3 force components + 3 moment components and assert norm <= 1e-6 * applied norm.\n\nPer-node extraction: model.mesh.nodes — node ids and coords; dofs = dof_manager.get_node_dofs(node_id) → 6 dofs [ux,uy,uz,rx,ry,rz]. ✓\n\nApplied load per node: F_prop vector at node dofs. ✓\n\nNow, the load: add_tip_load adds -2000 N at each of the NY+1=2 tip nodes → F_prop has fz=-2000 at 2 nodes. Applied wrench about origin: r×p for each. Fine.\n\nPlasticity check: alpha_max from element states (like ep diagnostic) and steps[-1].max_equivalent_plastic_strain > 0. For the -0.20 DC run: the preyield at -2kN/node gives uz≈-0.011 elastic; -0.20 m is way past yield → plastic. ✓ (the attribution runs showed plasticity at uz -0.0167 for VK; the CR 6k run lambda 0.94...). Hmm wait — will the DC run to -0.20 actually complete in 12 steps with max 40 iterations? The prior ep-edge-dc-full-direct run: the solve ran ~15s then failed at CHECKPOINT EMISSION — meaning the solve itself completed! ✓ (the traceback was in validate_nonlinear_checkpoint during checkpoint emission, after the solve). Actually — was it after the solve? The traceback: nonlinear_restart.validate_nonlinear_checkpoint — called during emit at the end. The receipt: EXIT_1 18.2s. The stdout was empty (the summary print never happened because the exception occurred before print). So the solve completed and checkpoint emission failed. With emit_restart_checkpoint=False → no emission → completes. ✓\n\nTiming: full direct ~15-18s. Full interp similar. Half runs ~half. Resumes ~half. Total module: preyield 12.6 + full_d 16 + full_i 16 + half_d 8 + half_i 8 + resume_d 8 + resume_i 8 + cancel_d 3 + reuse_d 8 + reject 0 + stopped_d 5 ≈ 93s + pytest overhead. ~120s. Set harness timeout 300 for margin.\n\nHmm, but caching: pytest may run tests in file order; each test requests cached solves. The cache is module-level, so a full run computes each expensive solve once. ✓\n\nOne issue: the cancel test needs the half_direct solve (cached, shared with split test) — fine.\n\nNow about comparing resumed vs uninterrupted displacements: tolerance. Both should converge to the same physical state; differences from convergence tolerance 1e-8 (residual) → displacement differences maybe 1e-9..1e-8? Plasticity: the resumed run starts from the exact half state (copied), so the material history is identical; the path targets coincide. Differences only from Newton convergence at 1e-8. I'll assert max abs diff <= 1e-6 (safe) — and report actual. Also lambda final: |lam_res - lam_full|/|lam_full| <= 1e-6.\n\nPlastic strain history: resumed steps (6) vs full steps[6:] (6): allclose atol 1e-8? The values are cumulative equivalent plastic strain at accepted states with identical control targets. Should match to ~1e-9. Assert np.allclose(..., atol=1e-8, rtol=0). Hmm — but if Newton converges to slightly different states, plastic strain could differ more. 1e-6 relative? Use atol=1e-8 might be too tight... I'll use rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-9? Let me think: eq plastic strain ~O(1e-3..1e-2). rtol 1e-6 → abs 1e-9..1e-8. Hmm. I'll assert <= 1e-6 absolute first run and calibrate if needed. Actually to minimize rerun risk, let me print diagnostic values in the failure... no — assertions should be final. I'll pick atol=1e-7 for plastic strain history (still very tight physically) and 1e-6 for displacements. If the run fails I'll see actuals and adjust once.\n\nControl values history: resumed step control values == full steps[6:] control values (both -0.1167..-0.20): allclose atol 1e-8 (control error gate). ✓\n\nNow the progress_callback signature: emit_progress(progress_callback, \"nonlinear_static_step\", \"nonlinear_static.displacement\", completed=..., ...) — what does emit_progress do with the callback? Let me check quickly to write the callback correctly.", "summary": []}
