{"id": "reasoning-9d39788e-3ed3-5b18-81b0-68db833f110c-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792347939240, "updatedAt": 1792347939240, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The postcheck is computed on `to_linear_displacement(u_final)` — the linear MPC coordinates — so the linear constraint equations are satisfied exactly (to roundoff). Fields: \"status\" (\"passed\"/\"failed\"), \"max_relative_residual\", \"relative_tolerance\", etc. So the assertion: `post[\"status\"] == \"passed\"` and `post[\"max_relative_residual\"] <= post[\"relative_tolerance\"]` — that's \"existing constraint limits\".\n\nNow write the continuation module. Also decide the alpha_max helper (from ep_isolation): max over element states of max|alpha|.\n\nOne more thing: the preyield elastic scale 1e4 multiplies yield stresses by 1e4 → elastic. ✓\n\nFor the cancel test: resume with progress_callback that cancels after 2 accepted step events. The callback: def cb(event): count += 1; if count >= 2: token.cancel(...). Then expect SolveCancelled. Note: emit_progress suppresses callback exceptions — but token.cancel doesn't raise in the callback; the raise happens at the next cancellation_safe_point in the solver loop. ✓\n\nAfter cancellation, assert inputs unchanged, then reuse the same inputs in a fresh resume → completes; compare with cached (\"resume\",\"direct\") result — the reuse run: same inputs (u0, s0 arrays — the same objects? I must reuse the SAME input objects per the brief: \"reuse same inputs\"). So the cancel factory and reuse use the same u_input/states_input objects. Design:\n\n```python\ndef _cancel_reuse_case():\n    half = _cached((\"half\", \"direct\"), ...)\n    m, lc, probe, grid = _build_ep_model(\"direct\"); add tip loads\n    u_input = np.array(half result displacements, copy=True)\n    s_input = copy.deepcopy(dict(half element states))\n    u_snap = u_input.copy(); s_snap = copy.deepcopy(s_input)\n    token = CancellationToken(); events = []\n    def cb(event):\n        events.append(event)\n        if len([e for e in events if e.event_type == \"nonlinear_static_step\"]) >= 2:\n            token.cancel(\"test cancellation after two accepted increments\")\n    try:\n        solve(... initial_displacements=u_input, initial_element_states=s_input, equilibrate_initial_state=False, cancellation_token=token, progress_callback=cb)\n        raised = False\n    except SolveCancelled:\n        raised = True\n    ...\n    # reuse same inputs\n    res_reuse = solve(... same u_input, s_input ...)\n    return dict(...)\n```\n\nWait — but the cancellation might arrive via a safe point BEFORE any step completes (e.g., at \"nonlinear_static.start\")? No — token.cancel is called only from within the callback after 2 step events. Before that, no cancellation. ✓ But careful: the callback fires after EVERY accepted increment; we cancel at the 2nd → the solve raises at the next safe point (during step 3). ✓\n\nAlso: does the cancelled run leave the MODEL in a corrupted state? We build a fresh model for the reuse run. ✓ (Model mutation doesn't matter; inputs are what we check.)\n\nHmm — one concern: the cancelled solve raises SolveCancelled — but does the exception propagate through solve_static_nonlinear public wrapper? The ep diagnostic expected SolveCancelled from dc_solve with status_callback... it used try/except around dc_solve. It exited EXIT_1 — hmm, was that because SolveCancelled wasn't raised or raised a different type? The ckpt probe batch EXIT_1... Actually ep-edge-ckpt-probe-batch EXIT_1 — the batch probe failed on checkpoint validation, not cancel. The cancel stage was never run (the detour was stopped). So the cancel behavior is unverified. Risk: maybe cancellation surfaces as a result with status \"cancelled\" instead of an exception? Let me check what happens on cancellation in the DC loop: cancellation_safe_point raises SolveCancelled. Is it caught somewhere and converted to a result? grep \"SolveCancelled\" handling in nonlinear_static.", "summary": []}
