d_constraint_transformation(K0, F_prop, model) — signature (K, F, model) returns 6-tuple ✓.\n\nNow the wrench: iterate m.mesh.nodes.values() — node objects with .id/.x/.y/.z ✓ (used in ep_edge_coupling grid_node).\n\nLet me now also handle: for the interp model — n_beam=3, beam nodes at 0, 1/3, 2/3, 1. At xb=1/3: sx = (1/3)/0.5 = 0.6667, ix=0, xi=0.6667 → masters [(grid[(0,0)], 0.3333), (grid[(1,0)], 0.6667)] ✓ interpolated.\n\nTime to write the file. Also include a module docstring documenting the adopted restart route.\n\nAbout line endings: module 1 is LF (\"new, LF\" per checkpoint). write_file will use whatever I give. Fine.\n\nOne more check — solve_static_nonlinear parameter `max_load_factor` default 1.0 — for DC irrelevant. For preyield force control: ramps to 1.0 ✓.\n\nAlso for the DC solve: does it need `record_increment_snapshots=True`? Yes for snapshot assertions.\n\nLet me also think about the \"reuse same inputs\" comparison to cached resume: the cached (\"resume\",\"direct\") ran with the same inputs (copies of half outputs). The reuse run uses the same u_input/s_input objects as the cancel run (which are themselves copies of half outputs). Deterministic → results should be bit-identical. assert np.array_equal? Both runs in the same process, same model construction, same inputs → identical execution → bit-identical. I'll assert np.testing.assert_allclose(..., rtol=0, atol=1e-14) to allow harmless nondeterminism... Actually if there's any nondeterminism it should still be ~1e-15. Use atol=1e-12, rtol=0. And lambda equal atol 1e-12.\n\nHmm — but wait: is the cached resume's model built identically to the reuse model? Both via _build_ep_model(\"direct\") + same loads. ✓\n\nAlso — the cancel test's fresh resume after cancellation (\"checkpoint still resumes\" analog): that IS the reuse run. ✓\n\nNow — one important verification: the half solve's result.displacements — is it the MAPPED full state suitable as initial_displacements for the resume? The R5 test passes pre.displacements as initial_displacements ✓ (same pattern).\n\nAnd initial_element_states: res.element_states — mapping element_id → state. ✓\n\nWriting the file now. I'll be careful about the load: -2000.0 per node (preyield used -2000/node; the DC cases in ep_edge_coupling also used add_tip_load(m, lc, -2000.0)). ✓\n\nFor _dc_case I need the load case added to the model: m.add_load_case(lc) — in ep_edge_coupling.add_tip_load. ✓\n\nAlso num_layers default 5 — _assemble_nonlinear_system called with 5 ✓ consistent.\n\nLet me write the test file.", "summary": []}
