{"id": "reasoning-ba1f6adb-6b80-52e8-b6b3-208f4d55c341-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792348097247, "updatedAt": 1792348097247, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The DC solves return `stopped_at_limit`, not `completed`. Interesting — the prior ep-edge-dc-full-direct run: the solve \"completed\" enough to reach checkpoint emission... or did it? The traceback was at checkpoint validation — which happens even for a stopped solve? Hmm. Actually wait — the ep-edge-dc-full-direct EXIT_1 traceback was in validate_nonlinear_checkpoint — maybe called at emission regardless of status.\n\nSo the DC run to -0.20 with 12 steps/max40/tol1e-8 stops at limit. What's the stop reason? Need more info. Let me look at the full failure details in stdout — the first failure block. Also 4 passed: preyield (2), rejected restart, stopped snapshots... and half[direct] got further (failed at wrench balance 53.85 vs 0.0145!) — interesting: half[direct] status was... it failed at the balance assertion, meaning status==\"completed\" passed for half[direct]?? Wait no — the failure for half[direct] is the wrench balance: `assert 53.85 <= 1e-06*14481` — so half[direct] COMPLETED (passed status assert, passed residual assert?) — wait, order: _assert_completed_dc_case checks status, uz, postcheck, snapshots, then residual, then balance. It failed at balance → status completed ✓, uz ✓, postcheck ✓, snapshots ✓, residual <= 1e-7 ✓, but balance norm 53.85 vs applied 14481 → ratio 3.7e-3, way above 1e-6.\n\nHmm! So half[direct] completed to -0.10, but the wrench balance is off by 0.37%. And full runs stopped_at_limit. Two separate issues:\n\n1. Full 12-step runs to -0.20 stop at limit. The brief demands \"-.20m full12steps/max40/tol1e-8\" with \"controlerror<=1e-8m\" and \"Independent residual at actual lambda<=1e-7\" — the brief asserts this is deliverable. But the actual run stops at limit. Hmm. Wait — but the earlier diagnostic run (ep-edge-dc-full-direct) — did it complete? The stdout was empty; the traceback at checkpoint validation. If the solve had stopped_at_limit, would it still emit a checkpoint? Probably yes (checkpoint of last accepted state). So unknown whether it completed then. That run was on the OLD source (before R3/R4/R5 fixes? It was run at 17:59, before the source repairs). Hmm.\n\nWait — but the brief says \"Deliver the original EP geometry direct2/interp3 beam elements, S355 canonical curve, -.20m full12steps/max40/tol1e-8\" — with acceptance \"Independent residual at actual lambda<=1e-7, controlerror<=1e-8m, positive plasticity\". If the full run stops at limit (can't reach -0.20 in 12 steps with max 40 iterations), then... the acceptance criteria can't be met at -0.20. Hmm, but maybe the stop is due to a solver defect that I should fix (genuine production defect)? Or maybe the stop reason is informative.\n\nLet me look at the details: what is stop_reason / failure_reason for the full runs? The result.info[\"stop_reason\"]. The test didn't print it. I need diagnostics. Let me check the stdout more fully for the first failure block — pytest shows the assertion but not the info dict.\n\n2. The wrench balance failure for half[direct]: balance norm 53.85 N vs applied wrench 14481 (lam*2000*2 nodes... applied force = 2*2000*lam ≈ 4000*lam; moment about origin ≈ 4000*lam*1m; norm ≈ 14481 → lam ≈ 14481/5657 ≈ 2.56?? Hmm: |(F, M)| with F=(0,0,-4000lam), M=(0, 4000lam*1, 0)... norm = sqrt((4000lam)² + (4000lam)²) = 5657lam = 14481 → lam ≈ 2.56. Interesting — at -0.10 the load factor is 2.56?? Wait, that seems high: preyield at lam=1 gives uz=-0.011; at -0.10, lam ~ 2.5-9 (softening past yield). OK plausible.\n\nBalance 53.85 → relative 3.7e-3. That's NOT roundoff. So the internal force wrench is NOT self-equilibrated to machine precision — or the residual at free dofs contributes: the independent residual passed (<=1e-7 relative to J^T F_prop — note ref_norm = |J^T F_prop| which at the mapped state includes the moment covector transfer... hmm).\n\nWait — actually, maybe my wrench balance has a bug: the imbalance g = f_int - lam*F_prop at SUPPORT dofs equals support forces, but at FREE dofs it's the residual (~1e-7 relative — small). The wrench balance sums r×g over ALL nodes: at free nodes g≈0 ✓. At support nodes: r×f + m. Sum + applied = 0 required. 53.85 discrepancy.\n\nHmm — is the shell element's internal force wrench not exactly self-equilibrated? For a corotational shell with plasticity... the internal force vector f_int satisfies: rigid-body virtual work = 0? For translation: sum f = 0 exactly (shape function property). For rotation about origin: sum(r×f + m) = 0 — this requires the element residual to be invariant under rigid rotation — true for corotational formulations to roundoff... but with 5-layer plasticity integration and the E4 PL shell — should still hold.\n\nWait — OR: the beam element contributions! The beam is connected via MPCs; its internal forces enter at slave dofs; the map transfers slave forces to masters via reduce_force with the moment covector — but that's for the REDUCED residual. The FULL f_int from _assemble_nonlinear_system at slave dofs — the slave dofs are dependent; their f_int entries are whatever the beam element contributes. In the wrench sum over all nodes, the slave node forces are included at the slave position r_slave = (x, -W/2, e). But physically the beam force enters the shell at the MASTER position through the lever arm... The full-space equilibrium: f_int_full = K-ish... the full internal force vector (with dependent dofs) is NOT self-equilibrated in wrench by itself — the constraint forces (Lagrange multipliers) balance it. The wrench balance must be computed on the REDUCED/system level, or the slave imbalance must be transferred to masters first (like transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters does for reactions).\n\nRight! The solver's own support reaction computation does exactly that: reaction_imbalance → transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters → support reactions. So my independent wrench must apply the same transfer: g_transferred = mpc_map.transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters(u, g), then wrench from g_transferred + applied. The transfer moves slave forces to master positions with the moment covector (A.T @ f_b), making the wrench consistent.\n\nLet me redo: balance = sum over nodes of (r×g_t + m_t) + applied. With g_t zero at slave dofs and masters augmented. Then the internal wrench self-equilibrium should hold to roundoff.\n\nHmm wait, but is that the right physics? The transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters: for each entry: f_b (slave force) → masters get w*f_b; moment_covector = m_b + A.T@f_b → masters get w*moment_covector. A = lever arm Jacobian at theta. This is the covector transfer (transpose of the map Jacobian) — exactly J^T. So g_t = J^T g in the relevant blocks... and wrench of J^T g + ... hmm, the transferred wrench: sum over masters of (r_m × w f_b + w(m_b + A.T f_b)). Is that wrench-equivalent to the slave's (r_b × f_b + m_b)? For the exact map: u_b = u_m + (R-I)r0. Virtual rigid rotation... yes — the transfer is designed so that the reduced system is in wrench balance (the map preserves rigid-body virtual work). So computing the wrench on the transferred imbalance should balance.\n\nAlternatively — simpler and cleaner: compute the wrench balance in the REDUCED space? No — wrench balance is a full-space physical statement.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe simpler: the support reactions from the solver (steps[-1].support_reactions) are already transferred. But supervisor wants independent computation, not output fields.\n\nOK so fix: apply transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters before the wrench sum. But careful: transfer uses u (mapped state) ✓ available.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters zeroes the slave dofs and adds w*f_b to master TRANSLATIONAL dofs and w*moment_covector to master ROTATIONAL dofs. The master rotational dof \"moment\" entries are generalized nodal moments — in my wrench sum I treat g[rot dofs] as nodal moments m ✓.\n\nAfter transfer, at free master dofs g_t ≈ residual (small) ✓, at support dofs = reactions ✓. Sum wrench + applied ≈ 0 to roundoff + residual level.\n\nHmm — but actually is the transferred system's wrench exactly the physical wrench? The physical structure: shell + beam connected at masters with lever arms. The beam's internal force wrench enters the shell at the master nodes with the lever moment — the transfer represents exactly that. ✓\n\nNow issue 1: full runs stopped_at_limit. Need the stop reason. Also — the half[direct] completed but half[interp] stopped_at_limit?? From the failures: half[interp] failed with 'stopped_at_limit' == 'completed'. And half[direct] completed. Interesting.\n\nWhat are the stop reasons? \"stopped_at_limit\" — the status; info[\"stop_reason\"] / failure_reason would say e.g. \"minimum_load_increment_reached\" or \"max_iterations_exceeded\". I need to see. Let me write a small probe... or better: make the test print the info on failure? No — run a quick diagnostic through the harness: a script that runs the full direct case and prints result.info (stop_reason, last control value, steps summary). ~20s.\n\nActually — wait. Before probing, think: is this a genuine production defect in my repaired source (R3/R4/R5 changes) or pre-existing? The prior ep-edge-dc-full-direct (old source, before R3 fix? — the R3/R4/R5 source repairs were made in the continuation run AFTER 18:02; the dc-full run was 17:59 — before the source repairs). So on old source the full run also... unknown status (checkpoint emission crashed first).\n\nHmm, wait — actually the checkpoint probes at 18:00-18:02 (ep-edge-ckpt-probe: DC -0.02, 4 steps) EXIT — completed. So small DC targets work.\n\nIs the -0.20 target reachable at all? The brief asserts it as the deliverable (\"-.20m full12steps/max40/tol1e-8\" with controlerror<=1e-8 and residual<=1e-7). The supervisor's decision says this is the contract. If the current source can't reach it, either there's a defect introduced by the repairs (R4 auto→work_conjugate changes the tangent; R5 admission; R3 selector) or the case genuinely needs more iterations.\n\nNote: the DC solve uses corotational_tangent default \"auto\" → with rotating MPC + no follower → auto resolves to... R4: \"rotating-MPC auto resolves work_conjugate even with follower loads; explicit rotated/consistent refused on the new route\". So auto → work_conjugate on this route. The work-conjugate tangent is the correct consistent one — convergence should be good (quadratic).\n\nstopped_at_limit with max_iterations=40 per step and 12 steps... The stop means: steps exhausted with cutbacks reaching min_step_fraction → \"minimum_load_increment_reached\"? That was the 6k attribution behavior (lambda 0.94, stopped_at_limit, 9 cutbacks) — adopted as valid partial equilibrium for FORCE control. But for DC to -0.20, stopping means the target wasn't reached — control error would be large.\n\nHmm wait — but half[direct] COMPLETED to -0.10 in 6 steps. So -0.10 reachable. Full to -0.20 in 12 steps stopped. The resume (6 steps from -0.10 to -0.20) also stopped_at_limit. So the -0.10→-0.20 range is hard: maybe near a limit point (snap-through)? In DC, a limit point in the load factor isn't a block (lambda is free)... but severe softening could stall Newton.\n\nOR — maybe there's a defect in the work_conjugate tangent for the rotating map (my R4/R5 repairs) that hurts convergence in the plastic range. The preyield controls pass (elastic, work_conjugate fine). The half[direct] to -0.10 completed (plastic). Full to -0.20 stops.\n\nHmm — what did the OLD source do at -0.20? The old source had auto→rotated (legacy) on this route? The R4 repair changed auto to work_conjugate. The ep-edge-dc-full-direct run on old source: crashed at checkpoint emission — but the solve portion... 18.2s total. If the solve had completed 12 steps, ~15s + emission ~3s. Plausible either way.\n\nI need data: stop_reason, achieved control value, lambda history, cutbacks. Let me write a probe diagnostic (owned: diagnostics/) that runs the full direct case with the current source and prints JSON: status, stop_reason, last control, steps (control values, iterations, residual norms, load factors), info keys. Run through harness (~40s with 120s timeout).\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider the wrench issue first, since it's independent. And for the stopped runs: also check whether \"stopped_at_limit\" is status with failure_reason \"minimum_load_increment_reached\" — in which case the achieved state is a valid partial equilibrium but the -0.20 gate is unmet — a real blocker for the deliverable. The brief says \"Fix genuine production defects if uncovered.\"\n\nAlso possible: the full run's problem is the FIRST step: from zero state to -0.0167 — no wait, half[direct] did -0.10 in 6 steps (steps of -0.0167) fine. Full 12 steps = same step size. So the full run stalls somewhere in -0.10..-0.20 — same range where the resume stalls. So the range -0.10..-0.20 is genuinely hard (limit point / severe softening), OR a tangent defect shows up only at larger rotations.\n\nHmm — wait, actually let me reconsider: half[interp] ALSO stopped (at -0.10!). And half[direct] completed. So interp is harder than direct even to -0.10. Hmm.\n\nWhat was the OLD behavior for interp? Unknown — the dc_split/dc_full stages were never run for interp (only dc_full direct, which crashed at emission).\n\nLet me get the actual data. Probe script: run full direct + full interp, print stop_reason, failure_reason, last control value, per-step iterations/residuals/load_factor/control, total iterations, cutbacks. Also run with corotational_tangent=\"work_conjugate\" explicitly (same as auto here) — no, same thing.\n\nLet me also print info[\"displacement_control_initial_value\"], and whether any step hit max_iterations.\n\nI'll write diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py and run it.", "summary": []}
