{"id": "reasoning-ba968b6c-032a-5def-aedf-616ea62e62f0-0", "sessionId": "99df4bb9-552f-17e2-4fbf-9a21a62c2ae9", "turnId": "turn-929c7b13b199714ef5c9c40d2b9ebe6a", "createdAt": 1792348174947, "updatedAt": 1792348174947, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Key finding: in the DC loop, when a step hits max_iterations, `failure_reason = \"maximum_iterations_reached\"` → the solve STOPS ENTIRELY (status stopped_at_limit) — there is NO cutback for max_iterations in the DC route! Cutbacks (min_step_fraction) exist in the FORCE control route (the 6k attribution had 9 cutbacks), but the DC route treats max_iterations as a fatal stop, rolling back to the step start and breaking.\n\nHmm wait — is that the intended DC behavior? \"maximum_iterations_reached\" → stop. So for the DC route, a step that needs more than max_iterations kills the run. The brief demands: \"-.20m full12steps/max40/tol1e-8\" completed with controlerror<=1e-8. With the current source, step 11 needs >40 iterations.\n\nSo the question: why does step 11 need >40 iterations when steps 1-10 need 7? A healthy Newton with a consistent tangent converges quadratically — 40 iterations means something is wrong (stall). Let me get the per-iteration residual trajectory. I can't easily instrument the solver... but I CAN reproduce the failing step manually: take the state at -0.1667 (from a 10-step run to -0.1667... or restart from the -0.1667 accepted state) and run ONE step to -0.1833 with my own Newton using the same pieces (assemble, reduce_tangent, factorize) — printing residual per iteration. That's a diagnostic script — doable but heavy.\n\nAlternatively — think about WHY it stalls. Look at the convergence criterion:\n\n```\nif residual_norm <= tolerance * reference and constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale:\n```\n\nreference = max(|ref_vec|, 1.0) where ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop). tolerance=1e-8. So relative residual 1e-8 — tight but fine.\n\nThe reported per-step residual_norm values (1e-5..1e-7) are the residual at the LAST iteration before the convergence check passed? No wait — the step record is appended after acceptance with residual_norm — the final residual... 1.36e-5 > 1e-8*reference? Unless reference is large: reference = |reduce_force(u, F_const + max(lam,1)*F_prop)|. F_prop = 2000N×2 nodes; reduce_force with the moment covector transfer at the mapped state... |ref_vec| could be ~1e4-1e5 (lam 2.8: 2.8*4000 = 11200 force + moment covector terms ~ 11200*0.04...). Hmm 1e4ish. tolerance*reference = 1e-8*1e4 = 1e-4. So accepted residuals up to ~1e-4! The reported 1.36e-5 <= 1e-4 ✓. So the effective convergence is relative 1e-8 against a reference ~1e4 → absolute ~1e-4. OK.\n\nSo at step 11, Newton doesn't get the residual below ~1e-4·... within 40 iterations. Stall or slow convergence (linear rate due to inconsistent tangent).\n\nHypothesis A: the plastic algorithmic tangent is inconsistent with the return mapping at larger strains (constitutive level) — but that would affect the force-control route too... the 6k force run had 9 cutbacks and stopped at lambda 0.94 — hmm, that was adopted as \"partial equilibrium valid, not a solver bug\" — but maybe it's the SAME underlying stall! The supervisor decided that was valid partial equilibrium though.\n\nHypothesis B: the rotating-map work_conjugate tangent has a defect that grows with rotation magnitude. At step 11, probe uz = -0.1833 → tip rotation ~? The cantilever bends: rotation angle ~ 2*sqrt(uz*L... for a plastic hinge-ish: rotation at tip could be ~0.3-0.5 rad. The R4 FD test verified the tangent at rotations up to 0.35 rad with EMPTY (elastic) states and it matched to 1e-6. So the elastic tangent is right. With plasticity: K_T from _assemble_nonlinear_system includes the algorithmic plastic tangent; the map's reduce_tangent uses J^T K_T J + geometric(u, F_int - F_ext) — the geometric term uses the imbalance... The FD test in R4 compared net_reduced_force including f_int from states {} — elastic. So the map+tangent machinery is verified elastic; the plastic tangent inside K_T is element-level, shared with the rotated route.\n\nHypothesis C: it's genuinely hard physics (material nonlinearity knee + large rotation) and 40 iterations is just not enough for the LAST steps, while the brief's parameters were chosen expecting them to work. But the brief (Astra) explicitly demands full12/max40/tol1e-8 completing with control error <=1e-8. Astra inspected the receipts... but no prior run ever completed -0.20 (the only attempt crashed at checkpoint emission BEFORE printing status — so its status was never observed!). Hmm — wait, actually: did the OLD source (rotated tangent on this route, since auto→rotated without follower pre-R4) complete -0.20? The ep-edge-dc-full-direct run: 18.2s, crashed at checkpoint emission. If the solve completed 12 steps, it would take ~12*7*0.4 ≈ 34s... it took 18.2s total. The current full direct run: 109 iterations ≈ 31.75s for both direct+interp (direct ~20s?). Hmm, hard to compare. The old run at 18.2s might have done ~60-70 iterations — consistent with ~10 steps × 7 iterations — i.e., it ALSO stopped around step 10-11 (uz -0.167) and then crashed at checkpoint emission! So the old source likely ALSO failed to reach -0.20. This is NOT a regression from my repairs — it's a pre-existing behavior... but the brief demands it work.\n\nHmm wait — but on the old source auto→rotated (different tangent). Iteration counts would differ. Can't conclude it completed.\n\nLet me think about what \"maximum_iterations_reached\" with 40 iterations means numerically. If Newton were converging quadratically, 40 iterations is absurd. So there's a stall — the iteration converges slowly (linear with rate ~0.9?) or cycles. A stall with a VERIFIED-consistent tangent (elastic FD) but plasticity active points to the plastic algorithmic tangent being inconsistent with the return-mapped stress — i.e., the element's tangent during iterations uses the trial state... \n\nWait — look at the iteration loop: `F_int, K_T, trial_states = _assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u, committed_states, ...)` — assembles at current u from committed states, producing trial states. K_T is the algorithmic tangent at the trial state. Standard Newton: consistent. Then convergence check: residual = reduce(u, F_ext - F_int). If converged: states_new = trial_states; commit. This is standard consistent Newton with return mapping. Should converge quadratically IF the algorithmic tangent is consistent.\n\nUnless... the shell element's plasticity tangent is NOT the consistent algorithmic one (e.g., uses the elastic tangent in some terms, or the corotational transformation uses the rotation at the START of the step...). \"work_conjugate\" corotational tangent — hmm.\n\nOK — rather than theorize, let me measure: reproduce step 11 manually with per-iteration residuals. Take the direct model, run 10 steps to -0.1667 (or restart from the -0.1667 state — cheaper: run to -0.1667 with 10 steps... that's another 20s), then manually do Newton iterations for target -0.1833 printing residual norms. Actually simpler: run the DC solve with num_steps=1 from the -0.1667 restart state with max_iterations=40 and a progress/instrumentation... no per-iteration hook exists.\n\nManual Newton script: I have all the pieces (used in the R4 FD test): build model, T, map, restart state (from a 10-step solve), then iterate:\n- u = full_u(q); F_int, K_T, trial = _assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u, committed_states, 5, tangent=True, kinematics corotational, work_conjugate)\n- residual = map.reduce_force(u, lam*F_prop - F_int) (F_const=0)\n- current = row_full@u; constraint = target - current\n- K_red = map.reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - lam*F_prop, None)\n- F_prop_red = map.reduce_force(u, F_prop); row_jac = row_full@J(u)\n- solve augmented, update q, lam; print residual norms per iteration.\n\nThis replicates the solver's iteration exactly (same formulas as the code above). ~20s for the 10-step prefix + a few seconds for the iterations. This will show the convergence rate and let me diagnose (e.g., residual plateaus at some level → inconsistency; or oscillation).\n\nAlso print: number of plastic layers active, whether residual stalls at ~1e-4·ref.\n\nLet me also reconsider — maybe the issue is the CONVERGENCE CRITERION vs roundoff: at step 11, the residual might stagnate slightly ABOVE tolerance*reference due to roundoff in the reduce_force (cancellation). reference ~ |ref_vec|: with lam 2.8, ref = reduce(u, F_const + 2.8*F_prop)... hmm wait ref_vec = reduce(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop) — at lam 2.8: 2.8*F_prop reduced. |F_prop reduced| ~ force 4000·2.8 = 11200 + moment terms. reference ~ 1.2e4. tolerance*reference = 1.2e-4. The step-10 accepted residual was 1.38e-7 — well below. So roundoff plateau at ~1e-4 seems unlikely for step 11 when step 10 got to 1.4e-7.\n\nLet me just run the manual Newton probe. Write diagnostics/ep_dc_step11_probe.py:\n\n1. Build direct model, add tip load.\n2. Solve DC to -0.1667 with 10 steps (max40, tol1e-8) → get state (displacements, element_states, lam).\n   Hmm — actually the 12-step run's step 11 target is -0.1833 = initial + increment*11/12. A 10-step run to -0.1667 has different step targets (-0.01667 each — same increment!). 10 steps × -0.01667 = -0.1667 ✓ same targets as the 12-step run's first 10 steps. So the state at -0.1667 matches.\n3. Restart: num_steps=1, target -0.1833, max_iterations=40 → confirm it fails (same as in-run).\n4. Manual Newton from that state with per-iteration prints.\n\nActually steps 3+4 both need the same prefix. Let me do: prefix solve (10 steps), then manual Newton for the -0.1833 step with prints. If manual Newton also stalls, inspect: print residual per iteration, |delta|, lam, plastic strain of trial states, and maybe the number of iterating/plastic gauss points.\n\nAlso — to test Hypothesis B (map tan